Transcript: Understanding the NetSuite AI Roadmap & Custom Bot Strategy
Introduction: The Big Picture
"Hello! Today we are looking at a document regarding the evolution of NetSuite AI between 2024 and 2026. The main goal of this analysis is to help you decide when to use NetSuite's built-in AI tools and when it is actually worth the time and money to build your own custom chatbot."


Step 1: What You Should Stop Building (The "Redundant" Tools)
"First, let's look at the features NetSuite is releasing that make custom work unnecessary. The document provides a comparison table to show where custom development is now 'Redundant'.


Don't build a search bot: NetSuite is releasing 'Ask Oracle,' a chat bar that searches data and performs tasks. Building a bot for simple searches is now a waste of time.



Don't build a chart-maker: The 'SuiteAnalytics Assistant' can already build bar charts and reports when you ask it, so custom code here is redundant.


Don't build a 'How-To' bot: The 'NetSuite Expert' is an AI help desk that knows all the official manuals. Unless you need to teach users your private company rules, you don't need to build this.


Don't build financial 'What-If' tools: The 'Planning Copilot' handles standard budget questions, like 'What happens if costs go up 10%?'.



Don't build standard accounting bots: Features like 'Autonomous Close' and 'AI Account Reconciliation' already handle the month-end busywork and matching bank records."


Step 2: When You SHOULD Build Your Own Bot (The "Gaps")
"So, when is it still useful to build your own tool? The document identifies specific 'Gaps' or 'Unmet Needs' where a custom bot is essential.


Unique Industry Logic: NetSuite knows how a generic company works, but if you are in a specific industry like Pharma or Construction with unique safety or legal steps, you need to code that logic yourself.


The External Detective: NetSuite mostly looks at its own data. If you need a bot to check external websites, shipping trackers, or competitor prices, you must build a custom bot to see that 'outside world'.


Chain Reactions: Built-in tools usually do one thing at a time. A custom bot is great for 'Chain Reactions,' where one sentence triggers five different actions—like receiving items, updating a budget, and emailing a vendor all at once.
+1


Private Knowledge: If you want a bot that acts like a smart employee trained on your private internal PDFs and secret operating procedures, you need to build that yourself.


Working in Slack/Teams: NetSuite tools live inside NetSuite. If you want your team to approve items directly inside Slack or Teams without logging in, you need a custom bot."


Step 3: How to Build It Safely (The Technical Framework)
"If you decide to build a custom bot, the document recommends using the technical framework NetSuite provides, specifically the 'N/llm Module'.



The Engine: Think of the N/llm module as the 'engine' NetSuite gives you to write your own bot logic safely.



Security & Cost: This allows you to build bots without paying for external OpenAI accounts, and it keeps your data inside NetSuite's secure environment.



Smart Strategy: To make the bot accurate, developers use 'Schema Narrowing'. Instead of letting the AI look at all 1,000 tables, you only show it the 2 or 3 relevant ones (like Projects or Tasks). This stops the AI from getting confused or making mistakes."


Step 4: Future Roadmap (2025–2026)
"Finally, keep an eye on what is coming in the next two years so you don't build something that will be released soon.


2025 - Project Health Meter: This will act as a proactive bot that warns you if a project is falling behind before it becomes a real problem.


2025.2 - Project Discussions: This tool lets teams chat inside a project record. The best part is that it has an API, meaning your custom bot can 'read' these chats and automatically write updates into the discussion.


2026 - Automated Status Narratives: This will automatically write summaries of project status, replacing the need for a bot to summarize task lists manually."

Conclusion
"In summary: Use NetSuite's built-in tools for standard math, reporting, and help-desk tasks. Save your development time for building custom bots that handle your unique industry rules, external data, and complex 'chain reaction' workflows."
